    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_a191ea40e11e95a315b6c24a3e62206f90964310eccd053392e4d4378ee3af71",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "On the critical line, the pole-cleared truncation function factors into a nonvanishing ξ amplitude times sin(θ(2t)+t log T), converting zeros into crossings of one continuous phase. At T=1 simplicity comes from the cited differenced-ξ theorem; for T>1 the added log T in the phase derivative gives transversality, its endpoint growth yields the ξ-count plus the linear-in-U correction, and monotonicity in both t and T makes each positive zero ordinate decrease as T grows. For a0 an additional linear factor rotates oppositely, so this proof does not transfer; the source presents its connection to the escaping real pair only as a presumption requiring numerical and analytic control.",
