    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_d6dd488d813bbe4afd387cc57aef620ae11f7e465c8674a980a62bb3e0045c1c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "For y≥1 the Maass–Selberg argument first rules out nonreal zeros away from the critical line, while positivity of the Eisenstein series for real σ>1 and reflection symmetry confine possible real zeros to (0,1). Pole clearing and antisymmetry reduce the right half of that interval to the positive level equation F(y,σ)=y^{2σ−1}f(σ)=1. The sign of the central derivative changes at y*=4πe^{−γ}, and strict increase of −f'/f makes F decreasing below the threshold but one-humped above it; consequently the level is not recrossed for 1≤y≤y* and is crossed exactly once to the right for y>y*, with reflection producing the second exceptional real zero.",
